\newlabel{ch:06}{{11}{174}{Claim: High-Interest Currencies Depreciate Enough to Offset Their Yield}{chapter.11}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {11.1}{\ignorespaces When U.S. interest rates are higher than Japan's, the dollar's forward rate sits below its spot rate. USD/JPY spot rate and implied one-year forward rate, 2016--2026. It quotes the exchange rate as yen per dollar, the usual market convention, which is the inverse of the $S$ used in this chapter. A rise in the plotted line therefore means that the dollar has appreciated and the yen has depreciated, so $S$ has fallen. The forward rate comes from covered interest parity. It lies below the spot rate because U.S. interest rates exceed Japanese rates, and the gap widened after the Federal Reserve began raising rates in 2022.}}{175}{figure.caption.40}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:06-usdjpy}{{11.1}{175}{When U.S. interest rates are higher than Japan's, the dollar's forward rate sits below its spot rate. USD/JPY spot rate and implied one-year forward rate, 2016--2026. It quotes the exchange rate as yen per dollar, the usual market convention, which is the inverse of the $S$ used in this chapter. A rise in the plotted line therefore means that the dollar has appreciated and the yen has depreciated, so $S$ has fallen. The forward rate comes from covered interest parity. It lies below the spot rate because U.S. interest rates exceed Japanese rates, and the gap widened after the Federal Reserve began raising rates in 2022}{figure.caption.40}{}}
