\newlabel{eq:06-cip-abroad}{{11.2}{176}{Covered interest parity (CIP)}{equation.11.1.2}{}}
\newlabel{eq:06-cip}{{11.3}{176}{Covered interest parity (CIP)}{equation.11.1.3}{}}
\newlabel{eq:06-cip-log}{{11.4}{177}{Covered interest parity (CIP)}{equation.11.1.4}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {11.1.4}Uncovered interest parity (UIP)}{177}{subsection.11.1.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{eq:06-uip}{{11.5}{177}{Uncovered interest parity (UIP)}{equation.11.1.5}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {11.2}{\ignorespaces Uncovered interest parity says the risky foreign route and the safe home route should pay the same on average. Uncovered interest parity, with the numbers of the worked example: one year, a spot rate of \$0.70 per Australian dollar, 1\% in U.S. dollars and 5\% in Australian dollars. The Australian route is not hedged, so its dollar payoff depends on next year's spot rate $S_1$. UIP says that investors expect the Australian dollar to fall to the forward rate of about \$0.673, so the two routes have the same expected payoff. Only the U.S. route is certain.}}{178}{figure.caption.41}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:06-uip}{{11.2}{178}{Uncovered interest parity says the risky foreign route and the safe home route should pay the same on average. Uncovered interest parity, with the numbers of the worked example: one year, a spot rate of \$0.70 per Australian dollar, 1\% in U.S. dollars and 5\% in Australian dollars. The Australian route is not hedged, so its dollar payoff depends on next year's spot rate $S_1$. UIP says that investors expect the Australian dollar to fall to the forward rate of about \$0.673, so the two routes have the same expected payoff. Only the U.S. route is certain}{figure.caption.41}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {11.1.5}The carry trade}{178}{subsection.11.1.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{eq:06-carry}{{11.6}{178}{The carry trade}{equation.11.1.6}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {11.3}{\ignorespaces The carry trade profits if the high-interest currency holds its value and loses if it falls far enough. Profit on the worked example's carry trade against the spot rate a year later. The trade earns \$40,000 if the Australian dollar is unchanged, breaks even at the UIP forecast of \$0.673, and loses \$65,000 if the currency falls 10\%.}}{179}{figure.caption.42}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:06-carry}{{11.3}{179}{The carry trade profits if the high-interest currency holds its value and loses if it falls far enough. Profit on the worked example's carry trade against the spot rate a year later. The trade earns \$40,000 if the Australian dollar is unchanged, breaks even at the UIP forecast of \$0.673, and loses \$65,000 if the currency falls 10\%}{figure.caption.42}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {11.2}Testing uncovered interest parity}{179}{section.11.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{sec:06-how}{{11.2}{179}{Testing uncovered interest parity}{section.11.2}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {11.2.1}The Fama regression.}{179}{subsection.11.2.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{eq:06-uip-expected}{{11.7}{179}{The Fama regression}{equation.11.2.7}{}}
\newlabel{eq:06-rational}{{11.8}{179}{The Fama regression}{equation.11.2.8}{}}
\newlabel{eq:06-fama}{{11.9}{180}{The Fama regression}{equation.11.2.9}{}}
\newlabel{eq:06-carry-fama}{{11.10}{180}{The Fama regression}{equation.11.2.10}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {11.4}{\ignorespaces In practice, high-interest currencies have tended to rise rather than fall, the opposite of what the theory predicts. Three readings of the Fama regression. The horizontal axis is $i^* - i$, the negative of the differential in Equation~\eqref  {eq:06-fama}, so each line has slope $-b$. Under UIP ($b = 1$), a high-interest currency falls by its interest advantage. Under a random walk ($b = 0$), it does not move, and under the typical estimate ($b \approx -0.9$, Froot and Thaler 1990), it rises. The carry trade's expected return is the vertical distance above the UIP line. At an advantage of 3 points, it is zero under UIP, 3\% under a random walk, and 5.7\% under the typical estimate.}}{180}{figure.caption.43}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:06-fama}{{11.4}{180}{In practice, high-interest currencies have tended to rise rather than fall, the opposite of what the theory predicts. Three readings of the Fama regression. The horizontal axis is $i^* - i$, the negative of the differential in Equation~\eqref {eq:06-fama}, so each line has slope $-b$. Under UIP ($b = 1$), a high-interest currency falls by its interest advantage. Under a random walk ($b = 0$), it does not move, and under the typical estimate ($b \approx -0.9$, Froot and Thaler 1990), it rises. The carry trade's expected return is the vertical distance above the UIP line. At an advantage of 3 points, it is zero under UIP, 3\% under a random walk, and 5.7\% under the typical estimate}{figure.caption.43}{}}
